            'title'   => 'Laser cutting paper and cardboard: invitations, boxes, models',
            'slug'    => 'laser-cutting-paper-and-cardboard',
            'excerpt' => 'Laser cutting paper and cardboard without a die: which paper to use, how to avoid scorch marks and fire, and which xTool laser suits invitations and boxes.',
            'blocks'  => [
                [ 'p', 'Laser cutting paper and cardboard lets you make lace-cut invitations, greeting cards, packaging prototypes and architectural models without a cutting die, so it pays off from a single piece and on short runs. Paper and card are cut at low power and high speed, with fume extraction and always under supervision, because they catch fire easily. For invitations with a colour print, the xTool M2 fits best because it prints and cuts in one job; for large sheets of board, choose the xTool P3 CO₂ laser.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'Why is a laser so good for paper?' ],
                [ 'p', 'The beam vaporises material along a narrow line and never presses on the sheet, so it doesn’t tear thin bridges or fine lace patterns. The file is the tool: a new pattern, a guest’s name or a different box size needs no die. At lower power the laser engraves, for example a monogram on kraft card, or scores fold lines. Typical jobs include:' ],
