                    'Card stencils for painting, airbrushing and decorating.',
                ] ],
                [ 'p', 'For thousands of identical pieces, though, a die at a print shop is cheaper per piece, and on light paper the laser leaves a slightly darkened edge.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'Paper, cardstock and board: what should you watch for?' ],
                [ 'ul', [
                    'Thin paper cuts very quickly but is light: air assist can shift it, and then the beam goes out of focus. Hold it down with magnets or weights outside the cut lines.',
                    'Cardstock, such as the 300 g/m² coloured or the 250 g/m² glitter card from xTool Selected, is stiffer and easier to handle. Test coated and laminated versions first, because the coating can melt.',
                    'Corrugated cardboard works well for packaging prototypes and models. Its air-filled flutes and glue make it ignite more easily, though, so after cutting check that no edge is smouldering.',
                    'Greyboard is the staple for architectural models and book covers. It is dense, so it cuts more slowly, often in several passes, smokes more and leaves a dark brown edge.',
                ] ],
                [ 'p', 'With a diode laser, colour matters too: white and blue paper absorb the 455 nm blue beam less well than dark card, so they need more energy or an extra pass. A CO₂ laser works in the infrared at 10.6 µm, which paper absorbs regardless of colour, and it cuts thick board faster.' ],
                [ 'p', 'Check laminates in the material’s data sheet: never put paper with a PVC layer under the laser, because it releases hydrogen chloride (more in our article on the sticker printer and cutter).' ],
                [ 'h2', 'How do you laser-cut paper without scorch marks or flare-ups?' ],
                [ 'p', 'Give the paper just enough energy to cut through: usually low power at high speed, focus exactly on the surface and, on thicker card, two fast passes instead of one slow one. Settings depend on the machine and the paper batch, so start with a test grid of several power and speed combinations. Some xTool Selected papers carry a QR code with ready-made settings, which camera-equipped P- and F-series machines read automatically.' ],
                [ 'ul', [
                    'Air assist blows away flame and smoke, but too much air moves a light sheet – match it to the paper weight.',
                    'Honeycomb beds and slats reflect part of the beam and can mark the underside; a support with a small contact area helps.',
                    'Cut inner openings first and the outer contour last, so the part doesn’t shift.',
                    'To limit smoke residue, cut unprinted sheets face down or cover them with low-tack transfer tape.',
                ] ],
                [ 'p', 'According to xTool, the CleanCut panel for the M2 protects the base plate from burn marks and scratches when cutting through, improves smoke extraction for a clean surface and smooth edge, and is designed for materials such as cardstock. The P3 comes with 17 metal slats, and for sheets 1 mm thick or thinner xTool recommends laying all of them so the material doesn’t vibrate or ripple.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'Fire risk: how do you cut paper and cardboard safely?' ],
                [ 'p', 'A brief flare that goes out as the beam moves on is common. The danger is a flame that doesn’t go out and a fire in the scraps under the sheet – usually from cutting too slowly at high power, cut-outs piling up on the bed or corrugated cardboard.' ],
                [ 'ul', [
                    'Never leave a running machine unattended, not even with flame sensors.',
